.TH XSD2CODE-GO 1 "2026-08-31" "xsd2code-go" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
xsd2code-go \- generate data classes from XML Schema, and infer a schema from documents or a database
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xsd2code-go generate
.RI [ flags ]
.IR schema.xsd ...
.br
.B xsd2code-go generate
.RI [ flags ]
.B \-\-database
.I dsn
.br
.B xsd2code-go infer
.RI [ flags ]
.IR document.xml ...
.br
.B xsd2code-go infer
.RI [ flags ]
.B \-\-database
.I dsn
.br
.B xsd2code-go version
.br
.B xsd2code-go help
.RI [ command ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xsd2code-go
turns XML Schema documents into data classes for C#, Java, Go, Kotlin, Swift,
TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Rust and C++. The generated classes deserialize conforming
XML through the target language's standard binding, in one call, and carry no
behaviour of their own.
.PP
With
.B \-\-postgres
\(em which is on by default \(em the same run also emits the DDL to store those
objects and annotates the classes to match it, so one object model serves both
the wire format and the database.
.PP
The
.B infer
command goes the other way. Given example documents it derives a schema that
describes them; given a PostgreSQL database it describes that instead, mapping
foreign keys to nested content and link tables to repeated content.
.PP
Both commands accept
.BR \-\-database ,
so a database can be turned into a schema, or straight into classes, without a
schema file in between.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B generate
Read schemas (or a database) and write data classes.
.TP
.B infer
Derive an XML Schema from documents or from a database.
.TP
.B version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B help \fR[\fIcommand\fR]
Print the overview, or one command's flags.
.SH GENERATE OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-lang " name"
Target language. One of
.BR go ", " csharp ", " java ", " kotlin ", " swift ", " typescript ", "
.BR javascript ", " python ", " rust ", " cpp .
The aliases
.BR cs ", " kt ", " ts ", " js ", " py ", " rs ", " c++ " and " cxx
are accepted. Default
.BR go .
.TP
.BI \-\-out " dir"
Directory to write into. Created if missing, along with any package
directories the language's layout requires. Default the working directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-package " name"
Go package, Java package, Rust module or JS/TS module name.
.TP
.BI \-\-namespace " name"
C# or C++ namespace. An alias for
.BR \-\-package ;
a dotted name becomes nested C++ namespaces.
.TP
.BI \-\-postgres [=false]
Add the persistence mapping and emit
.IR schema.sql .
Default true.
.TP
.BI \-\-table\-prefix " prefix"
Prepend this to every generated table name, so two schemas can share one
database without colliding.
.TP
.B \-\-ixmlserializable
C# only. Emit classes that implement
.B IXmlSerializable
with generated readers and writers, instead of classes that
.B XmlSerializer
describes to itself by reflection. See
.B C# WITHOUT REFLECTION
below.
.TP
.B \-\-stdout
Write every file to standard output, separated by a banner, instead of to disk.
.TP
.BI \-\-database " dsn"
Read the model from a PostgreSQL database instead of a schema file. See
.B DATABASES
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-db\-schema " name"
The Postgres schema to read. Default
.BR public .
.TP
.B \-\-db\-views
Include views and materialized views as well as tables.
.TP
.B \-\-db\-keys
Keep generated surrogate keys as content. They are dropped by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-target\-namespace " uri"
XML namespace for a model read with
.BR \-\-database .
.SH INFER OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-out " file"
Write the schema here instead of to standard output.
.TP
.BI \-\-target\-namespace " uri"
Target namespace. Defaults to the namespace of the first document, or to none.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-enum " n"
Treat a field with at most
.I n
distinct values as an enumeration. 0 disables enumeration inference. Default 12.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-enum\-samples " n"
How many values must be seen before an enumeration is inferred. Default 8.
.TP
.B \-\-strings
Give every value
.B xs:string
instead of inferring a datatype. Useful when the sample is too small for an
inferred
.B xs:int
to be more than a guess.
.TP
.BI \-\-database " dsn"
Describe a PostgreSQL database instead of XML documents.
.TP
.BR \-\-db\-schema ", " \-\-db\-views ", " \-\-db\-keys
As for
.BR generate .
.SH OUTPUT
.TS
tab(;);
l l l.
\fBgo\fR;models.go, xsdtypes.go;encoding/xml, plus json and db tags
\fBcsharp\fR;Models.cs, \fINamespace\fRDbContext.cs;XmlSerializer, EF Core
\fBcsharp \-\-ixmlserializable\fR;the same files;generated IXmlSerializable, EF Core
\fBjava\fR;one .java per type;JAXB (jakarta.xml.bind), JPA
\fBkotlin\fR;Models.kt;data classes over the JDK's DOM
\fBswift\fR;Models.swift;structs over XMLParser
\fBtypescript\fR;models.ts;readers over the DOM
\fBjavascript\fR;models.js, models.d.ts;the same, typed by declaration
\fBpython\fR;models.py;dataclasses over ElementTree
\fBrust\fR;models.rs;serde with quick-xml
\fBcpp\fR;models.hpp, models.cpp;pugixml
;schema.sql;with \fB\-\-postgres\fR, for every language
.TE
.PP
The Go, Python, Kotlin and Swift output has no dependencies at all: Kotlin uses
the DOM the JDK ships, and Swift builds a small tree on Foundation's XMLParser,
which -- unlike XMLDocument -- exists on every platform Swift runs on. TypeScript and JavaScript
need a DOM: browsers have one, and on the server
.B @xmldom/xmldom
registered as
.B globalThis.DOMParser
will do. Rust needs
.B serde
and
.BR quick-xml ;
C++ needs
.BR pugixml ;
Java needs
.B jakarta.xml.bind
and, for the persistence annotations,
.BR jakarta.persistence .
The generated files name their own dependencies in a header comment.
.SH C# WITHOUT REFLECTION
.PP
.B \-\-ixmlserializable
emits the same classes, with the same members, reading and writing themselves
through
.B IXmlSerializable
instead of being described to
.B XmlSerializer
by attribute.
.PP
XmlSerializer reflects over a type the first time it sees it and emits an
assembly to do the work. That costs a pause on the first document, and it is
why the attribute-based output cannot be published with trimming or NativeAOT
without warnings. The generated members name every property directly, so the
whole path is ordinary compiled code over
.B XmlReader
and
.BR XmlWriter .
.PP
The format handles for itself everything XmlSerializer would otherwise do:
inheritance, through virtual members a derived type overrides and calls
.B base
on;
.B xsi:type
, through a generated factory per base type;
.BR xsi:nil ;
enumerations, through a generated Parse and Format pair over the lexical
values;
.BR xs:list ,
split and joined in generated code, which drops the string companion property
the attribute format needs; and
.BR any / anyAttribute ,
kept as XmlElement and XmlAttribute arrays and written back as they arrived.
.PP
The classes keep the same shape in both formats, so switching is a
regeneration rather than a rewrite of the calling code, and the Entity
Framework mapping is emitted either way.
.SH MAPPING
.TS
tab(;);
l l.
\fBcomplexType\fR;a class
\fBextension\fR;inheritance, where the language has it
\fBrestriction\fR;a flattened standalone class
\fBsimpleContent\fR;a Value member plus the attributes
\fBsimpleType\fR with \fBenumeration\fR;an enum carrying each lexical value
any other \fBsimpleType\fR;the primitive it ultimately restricts
\fBgroup\fR, \fBattributeGroup\fR;expanded inline at the point of reference
\fBchoice\fR;optional members, documented as exclusive
\fBminOccurs="0"\fR;nullable, so absent and zero stay distinct
\fBmaxOccurs\fR>1;a collection, never also nullable
\fBlist\fR;one element or attribute split on whitespace
\fBany\fR, \fBanyAttribute\fR;raw XML, and name/value pairs
anonymous inline types;a class named for the element that owns it
.TE
.PP
C# is the exception to the nullability row above: XmlSerializer will not bind a
.B Nullable<T>
to an attribute, for any T, so an optional attribute is generated as a plain
value beside the
.B Specified
companion property that XmlSerializer does understand \(em the same shape
.B xsd.exe
produces. Optional elements are still nullable. The
.B \-\-ixmlserializable
format keeps that shape too, so the two formats stay interchangeable.
.PP
Kotlin, Swift and Rust have no inheritance that a data class or a struct can
use, so an extension's inherited members are written into each type that
extends it. The XML is identical to what an inheriting language produces; only
the generated type is flat.
.PP
Two decisions are worth knowing about. A
.B restriction
is flattened rather than subclassed, because it removes members from its base
and no target language can say that; XSD requires a restriction to restate what
it keeps, so nothing is lost. A
.B choice
becomes ordinary optional members, because modelling it faithfully would cost
the plain-object shape that makes deserialization one call; the exclusivity is
recorded in the generated documentation instead.
.PP
Anything that had to be approximated is printed as a warning on standard error.
.SH POSTGRES
With
.BR \-\-postgres ,
each class gets a table and a surrogate
.B id
\(em XML documents carry no dependable identity of their own \(em which is
excluded from serialization. Inheritance is joined-table: a derived type's
primary key is also a foreign key onto its base, which is EF Core's TPT and
JPA's JOINED, and what the DDL creates.
.PP
A single-valued complex member becomes a foreign key column. A repeated one
becomes a link table with an
.B ordinal
column, because document order is data and a set of rows has none. A repeated
primitive or an
.B xs:list
becomes a Postgres array. An enumeration becomes
.B text
with a CHECK constraint, rather than a Postgres enum type that every ORM maps
differently.
.PP
Every foreign key is added as a separate ALTER TABLE after the tables, so a
recursive schema has no ordering problem. The schema's own documentation is
carried into COMMENT ON statements.
.SH DATABASES
.B \-\-database
takes a URL
.RI ( postgres://user:pw@host/db )
or libpq keyword/value pairs
.RI ( "host=... dbname=..." ).
.BR PGPASSWORD ,
.B PGSERVICE
and
.I ~/.pgpass
are honoured, so a password need never appear on the command line \(em where it
would be visible to every other process on the machine and recorded in the
shell's history.
.PP
Reading a database produces one complex type per table. A single-column foreign
key becomes nested content: the row it points at, in place. A table that exists
only to join two others becomes repeated content on the parent. An array column
becomes a repeated value. A CHECK constraint pinning a column to a fixed set, or
a Postgres enum type, becomes an enumeration. Table and column comments become
.BR xs:documentation .
A table whose single-column primary key is also a foreign key onto another
table's key is a joined-table subclass, and becomes an
.B xs:extension
of it. Tables that nothing references are declared as the document roots.
.PP
What a relational schema cannot tell you, it does not guess: nothing records
which values were attributes, which were child elements and which were an
element's own text content, so everything comes back as an element. A schema
taken through
.B generate
and back is equivalent in structure, not identical in spelling.
.PP
A model read this way keeps the names it found: the table, the columns and the
primary key are facts about a database that already exists, and are not
re-derived.
.SH INFERENCE
A schema inferred from documents describes the samples, not the format. Read it
before treating it as a contract.
.PP
Structure is merged by element name, so an element appearing in three places
produces one type.
.B minOccurs
is 0 unless the child appeared in every instance of its parent;
.B maxOccurs
is unbounded if any single parent held more than one; an attribute is required
only if it was present on every occurrence.
.PP
Order stays an
.B xs:sequence
as long as every document's children are a subsequence of the order first seen.
Documents that genuinely disagree produce a repeated
.BR xs:choice .
.PP
Datatypes are inferred subtractively: a type stays a candidate until a value
rules it out, so one
.RB \(lq N/A \(rq
in a column of numbers demotes the field to a string. 0 and 1 are integers
unless something spelled out
.B true
or
.BR false .
Enumerations need repetition, not merely few values: a field whose every value
is distinct is an identifier, and is left alone.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on any error. Warnings do not affect the exit status.
.SH EXAMPLES
Generate C# with Entity Framework mapping and the DDL:
.PP
.RS
.EX
xsd2code-go generate --lang csharp --namespace Contoso.Orders \\
    --out ./gen order.xsd
.EE
.RE
.PP
Generate Go with no persistence mapping:
.PP
.RS
.EX
xsd2code-go generate --lang go --package orders --postgres=false order.xsd
.EE
.RE
.PP
Derive a schema from a directory of samples, then generate from it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
xsd2code-go infer --out order.xsd samples/
xsd2code-go generate --lang java --package com.contoso.orders order.xsd
.EE
.RE
.PP
Describe a live database as a schema, then as TypeScript:
.PP
.RS
.EX
xsd2code-go infer --database "postgres://localhost/orders" --out db.xsd
xsd2code-go generate --lang typescript --database "postgres://localhost/orders" \\
    --out ./src
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/xsd2code-go/examples/
An example schema and a document that conforms to it, installed by the
.B .deb
and
.B .rpm
packages.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR psql (1),
.BR xmllint (1)
.PP
Home page and issue tracker:
.UR https://github.com/chriswirz/xsd2code-go
.UE
.SH BUGS
Multi-column foreign keys are not turned into nested content: there is no
sensible reading of one as a single element, so the columns stay ordinary
values. Schema documents are never fetched over the network, so an
.B xs:import
that names only a URL is reported and its types become strings.
